From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winex11, win32u, user32, server: a pen's pressure and tilt reach Windows programs

A pen (a Surface's, a drawing tablet's) reached Windows programs only as
the mouse: the X server moved and clicked the pointer, and nothing more.
A drawing program asking for the pen -- Windows Ink's pointer messages
(WM_POINTERDOWN/UPDATE/UP with GetPointerPenInfo), as Paint.NET, Krita's
Windows Ink mode and Qt and GTK programs do -- got no pointer message,
GetPointerDevices listed no pen and GetPointerPenInfo failed, so every
stroke had one width. (sg-compositor 0.2.0+sg39 now hands the pen to
Xwayland as a tablet, whose X input device carries pressure and tilt.)

Now:
- winex11 finds X's pens (slave pointers named stylus or eraser, with an
  "Abs Pressure" valuator) once per process and lists them for
  GetPointerDevices. Every window takes the pens' own XI2 events (from the
  pen's device: X's core pointer events still come, and stay Wine's
  mouse); each becomes WM_POINTERDOWN, UPDATE or UP carrying the pen's
  pressure (0..1024), tilt (-90..90), barrel button and eraser in the high
  half of the hardware input's lparam. The core pointer event of the same
  X time is marked as a pen's (GetMessageExtraInfo's MI_WP_SIGNATURE), as
  Windows marks the mouse messages a pen makes, so a program taking the
  pointer messages leaves them.
- A full-screen program clips the cursor, and the clipping's pointer grab
  keeps the pen's own events from every window: while clipping, the
  thread takes the pens' raw events instead (raw events of a pen are never
  merged with each other or the mouse's).
- A window with a Wintab context takes the pen through X input 1, as
  Wine's Wintab always has: X gives a client a device's event one way or
  the other, never both, so that window's XI2 selection goes, and Wintab's
  events make its pointer messages too (selecting XI2 there had stopped
  Wintab's packets).
- The server queues a pen's message to the window under it (the driver
  names none over the desktop's own X window), or the window it went down
  on until it lifts, with WM_POINTERENTER and WM_POINTERLEAVE as it moves
  between windows; it makes no mouse messages of it (the X pointer does)
  and sends nothing more while the pen is still. A pen in range no longer
  keeps a touch from being the primary one.
- win32u keeps the pen message the thread last retrieved, sends it to the
  child window under the pen (or the one a stroke began on), and answers
  GetPointerType, GetPointerInfo(-History), GetPointerPenInfo(-History),
  GetPointerFrameInfo(-History), GetPointerFramePenInfo(-History),
  GetPointerCursorId, GetPointerDevices, GetPointerDevice,
  GetPointerDeviceRects (the pen's space is the screen's) and
  GetPointerDeviceCursors through two private NtUserCallTwoParam codes
  with fixed-size records (the same for 32- and 64-bit programs).
  GetPointerFrameTouchInfo(-History) and SkipPointerFrameMessages exist,
  so Qt turns its pointer support on.

Test: test/pentouch-gate.sh with test/penpointer-probe.c and sg-compositor's
-Dtest-tablet build (mutants SG_MUTANT_NO_PEN_POINTER in winex11 mouse.c,
SG_MUTANT_NO_POINTER_INFO in win32u message.c, SG_MUTANT_NO_PEN_QUEUE in
server queue.c).
